WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.534
-Thanks for coming back
on the show.

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:03.801
You know, I'm a big fan.
I want to congratulate you,

00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:06.501
first of all, on "Glass Onion."
Big hit movie.

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:08.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:11.000
Nominated for an Oscar.
-Yeah.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.734
-One of the biggest films.
Congratulations.

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:14.100
You got to reunite
with Daniel Craig.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.334
-I did.
Yeah, I got to reunite with him.

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:17.467
And it was weird
because it was like

00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.300
getting to know him
all over again.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.300
And he was such --
really such --

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.133
'Cause he's so different then
when he's filming "Bond."

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:27.200
When he films "Bond,"
I think he just has so much --

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.000
You know, it's so stressful,
and he's got so much weight

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.801
and so physical,
it takes a toll on him.

00:00:30.801 --> 00:00:32.701
-Did you get to hang out
with him much when he's Bond?

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:34.667
-We hung out --
No, not that much.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.334
Kind of, you know,
in between takes,

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:37.934
they kind of dragged him off,
and they probably were

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:39.968
icing him up and preparing him.

00:00:39.968 --> 00:00:44.267
He really physically puts
himself through it on "Bond."

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:45.701
-Didn't you break his leg
or something?

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:47.567
-I did not break his leg.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:49.367
-I thought you broke his leg
or something.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.334
-I did not break his leg.
-Yeah, you broke James Bond's --

00:00:51.334 --> 00:00:53.367
-For the record,
I did not break his leg.

00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:56.100
-But did he have a mishap
where his leg ended up broken.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.300
-His knee needed surgery.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.601
-What are you talking about?!

00:01:00.601 --> 00:01:02.534
-But it was not my fault.
-All right.

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:05.434
-We were tussling around,
and his knee popped and --

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:07.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.968
So, yeah.
And then we were tussling around

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.934
some more,
and then my nose was broke.

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:13.400
This was the train fight scene,
and it was really one of

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.067
the hardest fight scenes
I've ever done in my life.

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:16.501
-But I did read that,
though, right?

00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:18.801
The leg thing, then he had
to wait till it healed.

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:21.000
Then, you healed,
and you did the scene again,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.567
and you and broke your nose.
-Yeah.

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:24.601
It's like splattered all over.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:26.534
-He starts going, "I broke
his nose, I broke his nose!"

00:01:26.534 --> 00:01:28.234
I said, "You did.
But I'm all right."

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:29.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:31.167
I'm a wrestler.
I can do this.

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:34.234
And then -- yeah.
And then, there was a point

00:01:34.234 --> 00:01:36.200
we were filming "Glass Onion,"
and we were in Greece.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.934
And just --
It was a beautiful day.

00:01:37.934 --> 00:01:40.100
And we were sitting there in
beautiful, comfortable clothes.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.701
And I just looked at him,
I said, "Man, isn't it great

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:43.868
that we're on a film
where we're not

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:45.100
beating the [bleep]
out of each other?"

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:47.968
And he just started laughing,
but it was.

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:49.167
-Just to get to know each other.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:51.667
You're both good dudes.
-Thank you.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:53.734
-I want to talk about
"Guardians of the Galaxy"

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:58.100
because --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.734
You're beloved.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:01.701
-But you just confirmed
that this year will be

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:03.667
the last time
we'll see you as Drax.

00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:04.767
[ Audience "Awws" ]
-I think -- yeah.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:06.100
I think --
Yeah, it's weird.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.334
I don't know why it's,
like, news.

00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:12.367
I feel like it's -- you know, so
this is my seventh film as Drax.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:14.000
-Is it seven?
-Yeah, seven.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.934
And my third "Guardians."
It was like our trilogy.

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:18.100
That's kind of what we all
signed up for, to do a trilogy.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.400
And I think this is gonna be --
you know, this will be

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.567
the last version of our
"Guardians of the Galaxy."

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:25.567
And it's --
-Is it hard to walk away?

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:27.801
-It's hard, but it's time.

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.968
It's, like, time,
and it's the perfect exit.

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:33.100
We all had those perfect
kind of character arcs

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.100
and such a storybook ending.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.634
I constantly relate it to the
way I ended my wrestling career.

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:40.868
I ended it on a storybook note.

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:42.901
And I would never go back
and tarnish that.

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:44.033
And it's the same with this.

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.067
With Drax, I just got to end
the perfect way.

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:49.834
And I would never sign up
for another, you know, job

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:51.367
as Drax just to get a paycheck.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.133
It's just, I would tarnish that,
and I won't do it.

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:55.400
-Really?
I respect that.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.567
I love that.
-Yeah, thank you.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:03:00.434
[ Applause ]

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:02.634
You did such a great job.
We'll miss Drax.

00:03:02.634 --> 00:03:06.067
But I do want to talk about
your WWE career because

00:03:06.067 --> 00:03:08.434
we got to know you as Batista.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:10.434
-And last time
you were on the show,

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:12.701
we showed an action figure.
And that was cool.

00:03:12.701 --> 00:03:15.000
And we saw
that there's a new one out.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.934
I just want to show everyone.
Here's the new action figure

00:03:16.934 --> 00:03:19.434
for Batista out now.
Look at this.

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:21.834
And I like this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.534
Yeah.

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:27.367
You look good.
My question is this, though.

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:28.734
I don't know if you guys
can see this.

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:31.734
Can you zoom in?
Why is there a table lamp?

00:03:31.734 --> 00:03:34.267
[ Laughter ]
Is that a -- that's a lamp?

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:36.834
-That is a question
for the ages.

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:38.467
I don't know.
You know, this is so funny,

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:41.801
because this is kind of a repop
of my original one,

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:43.167
which is one
of my first action --

00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:45.234
my first solo action figure.

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:48.901
And for some reason, in that
one, they stuck a floor lamp in.

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:52.267
And I could never figure it out.
-Did you do something?

00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:54.033
-Batista complete
with floor lamp.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:56.133
-Yes, so, you have a floor lamp
in this one.

00:03:56.133 --> 00:04:00.133
-And this one has --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:03.667
And this one not only has a
lamp, but it has extra hands.

00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:05.467
And I don't --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:08.801
Am I -- do I take off my hands
or replace them with hands

00:04:08.801 --> 00:04:11.801
to remove -- to place --
to gently place the lamp

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:13.834
on the table?
-Did you do bits?

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:15.701
Did you throw lamps?
-Never.

00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:18.701
Nobody -- as far as I know,
nobody in the history

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:21.701
of professional wrestling
has ever used a lamp

00:04:21.701 --> 00:04:24.234
as a foreign object.
-Where would you find a lamp?

00:04:24.234 --> 00:04:26.067
[ Laughter and applause ]
Where would you plug it in?

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:29.267
-Why did they put a lamp
with my action figure?

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:30.434
-I love that
it's your thing now.

00:04:30.434 --> 00:04:32.467
-Somebody tell me.
I guess.

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:34.367
-That's your thing now.
-I do love --

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:37.701
I love some interior design.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:39.234
-That can't be why.
-I know that sounds funny,

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:40.868
but I do love --
-Yeah.

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:42.701
Well, that's interesting.
-I don't know.

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:44.834
-Yeah.
Maybe we'll see it some time

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:47.267
on an HGTV show
where you're like --

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:48.400
-[ Gasps ]
-You know what I'm saying?

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.000
And it'll finally
be revealed why.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.501
-Light bulb.
-Yeah.

00:04:51.501 --> 00:04:53.968
Literal -- literal light bulb.
Hello!

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:56.200
-And that's our next skit.
-Gotta call nine-fun-fun.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.634
Exactly.
-Somebody call nine-fun-fun.

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:02.033
-I want to talk about
"Knock at the Cabin."

00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:03.334
-Yeah.
-This is scary.

00:05:03.334 --> 00:05:05.567
I mean, this is a thriller.
M. Night Shyamalan.

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:07.367
One of the greatest out there.
-I love him, man.

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:08.901
-He's unbelievable.
-I just love him.

00:05:08.901 --> 00:05:10.367
-But you're great in the movie.
You guys --

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:11.934
He's a good director, huh?
-Yeah.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:13.868
He's a --
Yeah, he's a master director.

00:05:13.868 --> 00:05:15.701
But he's also
just a good human being.

00:05:15.701 --> 00:05:17.167
-Yeah.
-More importantly,

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:18.667
he's a good human being.
-Do you --

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:21.267
You work, obviously,
well together.

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:23.234
But how did you
get hooked up with him?

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:25.334
Did he find you?
Did you find him?

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:30.701
-Well, he originally --
So, my agent sent me an e-mail.

00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:33.167
It said that Night
wanted to talk to me.

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:34.834
-Yeah.
-And so, I flipped out

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.601
'cause I've lined up to work
with master directors.

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:40.400
And so, I flipped out.
I sent them back about

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.734
20 e-mails with 20 questions.

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:44.200
"What do you want to talk about?
What do you want to do?

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:45.767
Is there a film?
Is there a project?"

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:47.767
And they said, "No.
I mean, we don't know.

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:49.067
He won't say."
And he's --

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:51.534
You know, Night's very secretive
about his projects.

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:53.968
Anyway, they set up the call.

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:56.601
And I fanned out.
I fanboyed for about an hour.

00:05:56.601 --> 00:05:57.968
That's what it was.
-Yeah. Good.

00:05:57.968 --> 00:05:59.567
-That's what our call was.
-Why not?

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:02.300
-And then I was expecting him
to tell me about something,

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.567
I'm waiting for it,
waiting for it,

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:04.667
and it never came.
He said, "Okay.

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:06.334
Well, it was
great talking to you."

00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:08.634
And I got off the call.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.701
And I had to call my agents and
say he didn't mention anything.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:15.234
And so, anyway, we had about
two more of those calls.

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:17.901
And then, finally, I think
it was after the third one,

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:20.000
he finally says,
"Well, you know, I got a project

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.334
that I've been working on.
I think I have a role for you.

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:23.501
Are you interested?"
And I said,

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:25.100
"Yes, of course I'm interested."

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.834
So, he sent me the script.
I read it.

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:28.901
And it was very late at night.
And the next morning,

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:31.400
I called him and I said,
"I'd kill for this role.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.534
What do I have to do?

00:06:32.534 --> 00:06:34.234
Who do I have to kill
for this role?"

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:35.767
He said,
"You don't have kill anybody.

00:06:35.767 --> 00:06:37.567
You don't have to do anything.
I want you for this role."

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:39.934
He said, "Dave, basically,
you know, I've been talking

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.601
to you, and I wanted
to know who you were,

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.534
and, basically,
you are this guy."

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:45.434
So, yeah. He says,
"You're perfect for this role.

00:06:45.434 --> 00:06:48.434
It was tailor-made for you."
-Did you read the ending,

00:06:48.434 --> 00:06:51.234
and did you read the whole
thing, the whole script?

00:06:51.234 --> 00:06:53.834
-I did on this one, because I'm
very involved in the ending.

00:06:53.834 --> 00:06:56.000
-Yeah.
-But typically, I --

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.200
You're stooging me out.
-Well, I am because --

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:03.033
Again, I did a little research.
-I am guilty of not reading

00:07:03.033 --> 00:07:05.667
the endings of scripts of movies
I'm in because --

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:06.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:08.767
-"Glass Onion," I read
that you didn't read the ending.

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:10.667
-I didn't. No.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:12.701
I hope Rian's not pissed at me.
I totally didn't.

00:07:12.701 --> 00:07:14.734
-No, because you want
to be surprised?

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.100
-I do. I want to --
I'm a fan.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.701
I want to go -- if I'm not
involved in the end,

00:07:17.701 --> 00:07:19.567
I want to go,
and I watch it like a fan.

00:07:19.567 --> 00:07:21.701
-I know, but you're in it!
You have to read the script.

00:07:21.701 --> 00:07:24.067
-Yeah, but I kind of can
get a sense of what I need to do

00:07:24.067 --> 00:07:26.968
just by --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.968 --> 00:07:28.934
I'm not unprofessional.
I know my dialogue.

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:30.634
-You do.
[ Applause ]

00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:33.067
You're very professional.
-Yeah, I want to be surprised.

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:38.934
-Well, this one's another
M. Night scary thriller.

00:07:38.934 --> 00:07:41.968
How do we set it up for people
without giving too much away?

00:07:41.968 --> 00:07:43.934
-I'm so bad at this.
-I know.

00:07:43.934 --> 00:07:47.334
I should be good at this.
-So, I say that -- all right.

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:51.300
So, there's four strangers
who show up uninvited

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:56.334
to this cabin to convince
this wonderful family

00:07:56.334 --> 00:07:59.000
that they have to sacrifice
one of the family members

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.901
in order to prevent
the apocalypse.

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:03.434
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:06.801
-For real?
These four strangers show up

00:08:06.801 --> 00:08:08.501
to a family, and they're like,
"What's up?"

00:08:08.501 --> 00:08:10.067
-"Look, you guys are gonna
have to kill one of

00:08:10.067 --> 00:08:12.200
the family members."
-"One of you have to die --"

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.234
-"Or the world's gonna end."
-"Or the world's gonna end."

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:15.601
"So, choose."

00:08:15.601 --> 00:08:17.701
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.701 --> 00:08:20.200
-Oh, it's -- yeah.
It goes there.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.868
And it's --
Actually, it's unbelievable.

00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:24.033
It's a super fun ride.
It's insane.

00:08:24.033 --> 00:08:25.334
I don't want to even
show you what happens.

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:27.200
But, man, oh, man.
Fantastic.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.767
I want to show everyone a clip.
Here's Dave Bautista

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.701
in "Knock at the Cabin."
Check it.

00:08:32.701 --> 00:08:35.601
-You have to understand that
we cannot and will not choose

00:08:35.601 --> 00:08:37.734
who has to be sacrificed
for you.

00:08:37.734 --> 00:08:41.667
And just as importantly,
we cannot act for you.

00:08:41.667 --> 00:08:44.234
You cannot kill yourselves.

00:08:44.234 --> 00:08:46.367
-We're not choosing anyone.

00:08:46.367 --> 00:08:48.367
We're not sacrificing anyone.

00:08:48.367 --> 00:08:50.634
Not now, not ever.

00:08:50.634 --> 00:08:53.033
-Even if it means the death of
everyone else in the world?

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:55.133
-Yes.
Even if I believe the world

00:08:55.133 --> 00:08:58.667
was at stake, which I don't,
that's what it means.

00:08:58.667 --> 00:09:00.601
I would watch the world die
a hundred times over

00:09:00.601 --> 00:09:02.701
before having --
-Christ!

00:09:02.701 --> 00:09:04.901
Waste of time.

00:09:04.901 --> 00:09:08.033
They're never gonna
choose to do this.

00:09:08.033 --> 00:09:10.000
And I don't blame them.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.701
-Ooh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:13.701 --> 00:09:16.167
Dave Bautista!

00:09:16.167 --> 00:09:20.133
"Knock at the Cabin"
is in theaters this Friday!

